DismantleTable [SimTalk]
Sets the name of the Dismantle Table of the DismantleStation designated by <Path>.
Remarks
The Dismantle Table contains the names of the MUs, which leave the DismantleStation and its successors.
• Enter the path to the class of the MUs into the column MU, such as .MUs.Part, .MUs.Container
or .MUs.Transporter. Alternatively you can leave the entry empty to designate any MU class.
• Enter the number of MUs that are dismantled into the column Number. If you do not enter a number,
the DismantleStation uses the default value 1.
• Enter the number of the successor into the column Successor. If you do not enter a value, the
DismantleStation moves the mounting parts to the successor with the number 1 for the setting
Dismantle mode > Detach MUs.
For the setting Dismantle Mode > Create MUs it moves the MUs to the successor to which the main
MU moves.
Note
If you enter a MU Target the Worker carries all parts to this target.
If you do not enter a MU Target, the Dismantle Table or the setting Main MU to successor with
number determine it.
Depending on what you select from the drop-down list Dismantle Table: Create MUs or Detach MUs, the
DismantleStation creates the corresponding MUs or searches the table for the successor to which it moves
it. In the latter case the Dismantle Table may contain more information than is required for moving the
MUs.
If you only want to unload those mounting parts from the main part which you type into the Dismantle
Table, select Sequence > MUs exiting independent of other MUs or Main MU after other MUs and the
Dismantle mode > Detach MUs. Always enter a valid MU class into the column MU and a positive number
into the column Number.
DismantleTable [SimTalk]
11-1860 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To automatically move all parts according to their MU class to a successor:
• Type in any MU class into the column MU of the Dismantle Table.
• Type in the value -1 into the column Number to designate all remaining parts of this class.
• Type in the number of the desired successor into the column Successor.
• Repeat this for additional MU classes.
To automatically move all parts to a successor:
• Leave the column MU in the Dismantle Table empty to designate any MU class.
• Type in the value -1 into the column Number to designate all remaining parts of this class.
• Type in the number of the desired successor into the column Successor.
DismantleTable [SimTalk]
Plant Simulation Help 11-1861
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
If the dismantle table is inherited, inheritance is not automatically deactivated when you write to cells
of the dismantle table. For this reason you will change the inherited table during an assignment. To
deactivate inheritance, assign the dismantle table to itself.
DismantleStation.DismantleTable := DismantleStation.DismantleTable  
//  deactivates inheritance
Type
Read-only attribute
Syntax
<Path>.DismantleTable:table
Assignment Value
You can assign a value of data type table.
Examples
var DismList:table[object,integer,integer]
DismList.create
DismList.writeRow(1,1, .MUs.MyPart,1,1)
DismList.writeRow(1,2, .MUs.MyPart,2,1)
MyDismantleStation.DismantleTable := DismList
// You can use the following example in an entrance control 
// where 'EntranceCtrlBeforeActions' is set to true.
?.DismantleList := ?.DismantleList // Deactivates inheritance. 
// (For performance reasons, we recommend to do this in the dialog of the 
object.)
?.DismantleList[2, 1] := @.AmountOfParts
